Q: What are the use cases of WeakMap and WeakSet in JavaScript? A: <ol>
<li><p>What problems do WeakMap and WeakSet solve?</p>
<p>If the only reference to an object is within a WeakMap or WeakSet, the garbage collector is free to destroy the object and reclaim that memory.</p>
</li>
<li><p>How are they different from Map and Set in practical use?</p>
<pre><code>Feature                        Map / Set                    WeakMap / WeakSet
</code></pre>
<p>Reference Type                                  Strong (prevents GC)                              Weak (allows GC)</p>
<p>Key Types                               Anything (primitives/objects)                               Objects only</p>
<p>Iterability                                    Yes (forEach, keys, values)                            No (cannot be iterated)</p>
<p>Size Property                                     .size available                                          No .size property</p>
<p>Clearing                                      .clear() method                                  No .clear() (let it go out of scope)</p>
</li>
<li><p>What are some common real-world use cases (e.g., caching, DOM handling, etc.)?</p>
<p>A. Storing Private Metadata (DOM Handling)</p>
<p>If you have a UI library that adds metadata to DOM elements (e.g., an &quot;event listener count&quot; or &quot;initial state&quot;), storing this in a regular <code>Map</code> is dangerous. If you remove the DOM node from the document, the <code>Map</code> still holds a reference to it, preventing the browser from freeing up that memory. A <code>WeakMap</code> solves this: when the node is deleted from the DOM, the entry in the <code>WeakMap</code> is automatically removed.</p>
<h4>B. Caching Results</h4>
<p>If you have a function that performs heavy calculations on object inputs, you can use a <code>WeakMap</code> to cache the results. If the input object is no longer used elsewhere in your app, the cache entry is automatically deleted, preventing the cache from growing indefinitely.</p>
</li>
<li><p>Simple Examples</p>
<p>Using <code>WeakMap</code> for Private Data</p>
<p>Imagine you want to associate data with an object without modifying the object itself:</p>
<pre><code>const userMetadata = new WeakMap();

function trackUserClicks(userElement) {
    let clicks = userMetadata.get(userElement) || 0;
    userMetadata.set(userElement, clicks + 1);
}

let button = document.querySelector(&quot;#submit&quot;);
trackUserClicks(button);

// Later, when the button is removed from the DOM:
button = null; 
</code></pre>
<p>Using <code>WeakSet</code> to track &quot;Seen&quot; Objects</p>
<p>Use <code>WeakSet</code> to track objects that have already been processed without creating a persistent reference to them:</p>
<pre><code>const processedObjects = new WeakSet();

function process(obj) {
    if (processedObjects.has(obj)) {
        return; // Already handled
    }

    // ... do heavy work ...

    processedObjects.add(obj);
}

let data = { id: 1 };
process(data);

// When data is set to null, it can be garbage collected
// even though it was added to processedObjects.
data = null; 
</code></pre>
</li>
</ol>
